<Guide lang="en" slug="accessible-home-spain" toc={toc} faq={faq} dienst="renovatie" ctaTitle="Is your home ready for the next twenty years?" ctaText="Send us photos or a floor plan and tell us what you find difficult. You get a proposal per change, so you can decide what comes first.">
  <h2 id="short">Short answer</h2>
  <p>Adapting a home for later life means you can still live there safely with less strength, a walking frame or after an operation. The key changes are <strong>removing steps and thresholds, a walk-in shower, grab rails, a bedroom and bathroom on the ground floor and, if needed, a stairlift</strong>. Grab rails, handrails and good lighting are small jobs with a big effect. A walk-in shower is a medium-sized job. A downstairs bedroom or a lift is a major investment.</p>
  <p>The best moment is when you are renovating anyway, or when you buy. If you are replacing a bathroom now, making it step-free costs little extra.</p>

  <h2 id="typical">Why homes in the Axarquía are tricky</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Stairs everywhere</strong>: village houses are narrow and tall, with the living room on the first floor and bedrooms or the roof terrace above. Fincas sit on slopes, with steps from the parking area to the front door.</li>
    <li><strong>High shower trays and baths</strong>: many older bathrooms have a bath or a raised shower tray that is hard to step in and out of.</li>
    <li><strong>Thresholds and level changes</strong>: between rooms, onto the terrace and at the front door. Often just a few centimetres, exactly enough to trip over.</li>
    <li><strong>Slippery tiles</strong>: glossy floor tiles and outdoor tiles around the pool get slippery when wet.</li>
    <li><strong>Narrow doors</strong>: 70 cm interior doors are common, while a wheelchair or walking frame needs 80 to 90 cm.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="works">The changes at a glance</h2>
  <h3>Bathroom</h3>
  <p>A level-access shower with a non-slip floor, a fixed or fold-down shower seat, grab rails by the shower and toilet, and a thermostatic tap against scalding. A slightly higher toilet (around 46 to 48 cm) makes standing up easier. Have the door open outwards or fit a sliding door, so you can always reach someone who has fallen.</p>
  <h3>Floors and thresholds</h3>
  <p>Remove thresholds, smooth out small level changes with a gentle slope and, where needed, lay new non-slip flooring. Outside, a ramp or wider, lower steps with a handrail on both sides help.</p>
  <h3>Living on one floor</h3>
  <p>If a bedroom with its own bathroom can be created downstairs, you no longer need the stairs every day. In a village house this is often the biggest change, but also the most valuable.</p>
  <h3>Small things with a big effect</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Handrails on both sides of every staircase.</li>
    <li>Good lighting with motion sensors in the hall, on the stairs and in the bathroom.</li>
    <li>Switches and sockets at an easy height.</li>
    <li>Lever taps and lever handles instead of knobs.</li>
    <li>An alarm button or smart doorbell, so family or a neighbour can keep an eye out.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="costs">What is costly and what is not</h2>
  <p>Every house is different, so rather than amounts we show a price class. That way you can quickly see where a little money goes a long way and what is a bigger investment.</p>
