  <Todo label="controle van de prijsklassen (vooral traplift en huislift) door het bouwbedrijf." />
  <p>More about a new bathroom in <a href="/en/costs/bathroom-renovation/">bathroom renovation</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="stairlift">Stairlift, platform lift or home lift</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Stairlift</strong>: a seat that runs along a rail on the stairs. Cheapest on a straight staircase; with a bend or a spiral the rail has to be made to measure. The stairs stay usable for others.</li>
    <li><strong>Outdoor stairlift</strong>: for the steps from the street or parking area to the front door, which at fincas and village houses is often the real problem. Choose a model that resists sun, rain and salt.</li>
    <li><strong>Platform lift</strong>: a platform for a wheelchair, for a short height difference such as a terrace or a few steps at the entrance.</li>
    <li><strong>Home lift</strong>: a small lift between floors. More expensive and often needs a permit, but in a tall village house sometimes the only way to keep every floor usable.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>With any lift, ask about maintenance and a local repair service. A lift that has broken down while you are away is not something you want to discover when you return. If your house is checked through <a href="/en/key-holding/">key holding</a>, have the lift tested on every visit.</p>

  <h2 id="rules">Permits, VAT and the community of owners</h2>
  <p>Interior work such as a new bathroom or removing thresholds falls under a simple notification (<em>declaración responsable</em>) in most municipalities. A home lift, an opening in a load-bearing wall or a ramp on the public road usually needs a permit with drawings by an architect. We work with a regular architect and gestor for this.</p>
  <p>Renovation work on a home for private use can, under conditions, be charged at 10% VAT instead of 21%. Some aids for people with a disability qualify for an even lower rate. Ask your builder or gestor what applies to you.</p>
  <p>If you live in an apartment or urbanisation with a <em>comunidad</em>, the law requires the community to carry out accessibility works in the shared areas if a resident over 70 or with a disability asks for them, up to an amount of twelve monthly fees.</p>

  <h2 id="approach">How to go about it</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Walk through the house with a critical eye</strong>: where do you step up, where do you hold on, where is it dark or slippery? Note it room by room.</li>
    <li><strong>Start with the bathroom and the entrance</strong>: that is where most falls happen and where you gain the most.</li>
    <li><strong>Combine with other work</strong>: if a new bathroom, floor or wiring is planned anyway, make it step-free at the same time.</li>
    <li><strong>Ask for a quote per change</strong>: then you can choose what to do now and what can wait a few years.</li>
    <li><strong>Have it done while you are away</strong>: with weekly photos and payment per completed stage. Read how that works in <a href="/en/guides/renovating-from-abroad-spain/">renovating from abroad</a>.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Still looking for a house? Think now about what it will be like in fifteen years. In <a href="/en/guides/finca-apartment-or-village-house/">finca, apartment or village house</a> we compare the property types, including for <a href="/en/guides/finca-apartment-or-village-house/retirees/">retirees</a>.</p>
</Guide>
